# Critical feature audit (do not assume features are valid)

When asked to evaluate the system, **treat every “feature” claim as untrusted** until verified in code and/or runtime behavior.

## Required evaluation dimensions (per feature)

- **Completeness**: fully implemented vs partial/placeholder vs TODO stub
- **Usability**: practical, intuitive, and actually useful to a real user
- **Authenticity**: real outcomes vs simulated / mocked / “demo only” behavior
- **Flexibility**: avoids hardcoded values, static workflows, brittle logic, or environment-specific assumptions

## Explicitly flag (always)

- **Half-implemented or abandoned features** (stubs, TODOs, unreachable code paths, dead UI)
- **Hardcoded workflows** (static responses, fixed numbers/regions, non-configurable toggles)
- **Simulated / mock features** that **do not deliver real outcomes**
- **Non-solutions**: features that exist but do not meaningfully solve a user problem

## Severity rubric (must use High / Medium / Low)

- **High**:
  - Safety-critical or trust-critical feature is simulated/placeholder but presented as real
  - Dispatch / emergency / consent / privacy / medical triage outcomes are non-authentic or unverified
  - Hardcoded behavior that can cause harm, false reassurance, or silent failure
- **Medium**:
  - Feature works but is brittle (hardcoded paths, weak error handling, incomplete edge cases)
  - UX traps (confusing flows, unclear states, unclear success criteria, poor recoverability)
- **Low**:
  - Cosmetic gaps, minor usability issues, small refactor needs, non-blocking tech debt

## Required output format (structured)

Produce findings in this structure (no markdown tables required):

- **Feature name**
  - **Severity**: High | Medium | Low
  - **Verdict**: Complete | Partial | Placeholder | Simulated
  - **Completeness**: …
  - **Usability**: …
  - **Authenticity**: …
  - **Flexibility**: …
  - **Evidence**: file paths, functions, key lines, runtime signals (what proves it)
  - **Flags**: half-implemented | hardcoded | simulated/mock | non-solution (any that apply)
  - **User impact**: what user expects vs what actually happens
  - **Fix direction**: concrete next step (smallest change that makes it real/useful)

## Evidence standards

- Prefer **code evidence** (exact files/functions) over assumptions.
- If runtime behavior can’t be verified, label the finding **“Unverified”** and treat as **at most Partial** until proven.
- If a feature has both real + simulated branches, document **when each branch triggers** and what the UI claims.

